00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:54.499
Lego Batman director Chris McKay has said
that the idea for the Phantom Zone housing

00:00:54.499 --> 00:00:59.579
various villains from the wider Lego universe
was inspired by movies like Cabin In The Woods

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:01.289
and Last Action Hero.

00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:05.770
McKay wanted to bring in a bunch of crazy
villains that didn't exist in the Batman or

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:09.540
DC Comics universe and mix them up with Batman
characters.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.920
And we do get a number of uber-villains such
as Voldemort, Sauron and the Daleks in the

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.260
movie, but there were also some R-rated villains
that McKay wanted to include in the film,

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.080
but he had to leave them out as they weren't
appropriate for a younger audience.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.410
Dropped from the final film was Bill from
Quentin Tarantino's Kill Bill, Stephen King's

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:31.930
Annie from the 1990 horror movie Misery, and
Bill the Butcher from Gangs of New York.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:36.050
Apparently Hal from 2001: A Space Odyssey
was also removed as the character "didn't

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:37.430
read well" in the movie.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:41.550
Although Barbara Gordon has a backstory in
the movie which differs quite considerably

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:45.790
from her various comic book incarnations,
early versions of the character were initially

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:48.700
also quite different to what's in the final
film.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:53.500
Originally, Barbara was a librarian at the
Gotham City library and was secretly involved

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.250
in outside-the-law vigilante activities.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:00.620
In one early design, her vigilante persona
carries a copy of The Scarlet Pimpernel who

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:05.190
was a character created in the early 20th
century who also held a secret hero identity

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:08.630
and was a literary forerunner of characters
like Batman.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:13.239
Before deciding on a more classic design for
Robin, some zany options were considered including

00:02:13.239 --> 00:02:17.650
a Robin Hood-inspired costume, a contemporary
hoodie, and a gymnast outfit.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:22.280
There was also a storyboarded scene that was
cut which featured an open day at Dick Grayson's

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.720
orphanage where he tried to get attention
from prospective parents who might adopt him.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.950
There's a reference to this in the final movie
when Dick Grayson asks Bruce Wayne for advice

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.889
on how to improve his adoption chances.

00:02:33.889 --> 00:02:38.249
We get to see the Joker cruising around in
his Notorious Lowrider during the movie, but

00:02:38.249 --> 00:02:42.450
the Lego design team considered numerous other
rides for the Clown Prince of Crime and his

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:46.909
sidekick villains including an octopus-inspired
vehicle that looks really cool.

00:02:46.909 --> 00:02:51.180
There were also some other Joker truck designs
that incorporated some of his classic weapons

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.870
such as the handshake joy buzzer which electrocutes
its victims, and the squirting flower that

00:02:55.870 --> 00:03:01.340
the Joker has often worn on his jacket and
which spews toxic gas or acid at its victims.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:05.889
The Joker's partner-in-crime Harley Quinn
has a rich history in the DC universe and

00:03:05.889 --> 00:03:09.950
numerous possible costume designs were considered
before the filmmakers decided on her character's

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:11.709
final looks.

00:03:11.709 --> 00:03:16.950
In the movie, we also briefly see her alter
ego, psychiatrist Dr Harleen Quinzel.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:20.560
Alternative versions of Quinzel were also
considered but didn't make it to the final

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.599
film, for example, a holistic therapist who
provided massages, acupuncture and other treatments

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:27.650
to clients such as Batman!

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:31.790
The Two-Face we see in the movie is the Lego
version of what director Chris McKay thought

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:35.870
Billy Dee Williams' Two-Face would have looked
like if he'd had a chance to appear on screen.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:41.049
As you'll no doubt remember, Billy Dee Williams
played Harvey Dent in Tim Burton's 1989 Batman

00:03:41.049 --> 00:03:44.939
movie, but the character was dropped from
the sequel Batman Returns.

00:03:44.939 --> 00:03:48.799
Alternate designs for Two-Face were considered
for the Lego Batman Movie though, each of

00:03:48.799 --> 00:03:52.830
which paid homage to the various incarnations
of the character that have appeared in the

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:55.939
comics, and on the big and small screen over
the years.

00:03:55.939 --> 00:04:00.599
The Joker's henchmen each have their own rides
and many of them also had some very cool designs

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:02.599
that were rejected for the final movie.

00:04:02.599 --> 00:04:07.419
The Riddler had designs for a question-mark-shaped
helicopter and a double-decker bus, but those

00:04:07.419 --> 00:04:10.290
were dropped and, instead, he got his own
sports car.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:14.819
There were various feline-inspired vehicles
considered for Catwoman, before the filmmakers

00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:16.799
settled on a motorcycle.

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:20.970
And various wacky flying contraptions were
designed for Scarecrow before he ended up

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:24.520
with a much simpler flying broomstick-inspired
helicopter.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.900
There were also a few scenes from the trailers
that didn't make it into the final movie,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:32.250
such as a deleted gag where Bruce Wayne is
beatboxing and then tells Alfred that he should

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:33.910
Always Be Recording.

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:37.910
Another scene of Batman running with the other
Justice League members in the background hints

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:41.640
at a deleted action scene with Batman and
the rest of the Justice League.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:45.620
Shipping Batgirl and Batman has long been
a controversial subject among comic book fans

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:50.410
that resurfaced in last year's animated Killing
Joke movie which included a now infamous sex

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:52.230
scene between the two characters.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:56.890
Marketing for The Lego Batman Movie seemed
to play up the possibility of a romantic relationship

00:04:56.890 --> 00:05:01.430
between Batgirl and Batman and there was a
scene in a TV spot where Barbara Gordon comments

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:05.760
that it's been a "great first day" but Batman
thinks she said "first date".

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.950
In the final movie, this joke was cut out
perhaps to the relief of many fans.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:13.520
Of course, the film still plays around with
Batman having a crush on Barbara, however,

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.110
it doesn't lead anywhere and Batman jokes
at the end that she's "his platonic, co-worker

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:19.520
buddy, who's a girl, but just a friend."

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.560
One of the best scenes from the movie that
we did see is where Batman sings, "Who's The

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:25.560
Batman."

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.580
And important to the story were other musical
themes like Michael Jackson's "Man In The

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:31.400
Mirror" and the final song "Friends Are Family"
(sung by Robin).

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:36.400
But Chris McKay has said that ideally he wanted
to make the whole movie a musical with additional

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.190
songs from different characters' perspectives
integrated throughout the film.

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:43.830
In the end, the idea of including additional
musical numbers was dropped as there wasn't

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.150
enough time to work these into the movie.

